1
00:00:00,664 --> 00:00:26,423
(Singing)

2
00:00:26,423 --> 00:00:30,327
This song is almost 900 years old.

3
00:00:30,327 --> 00:00:33,330
It was written by Comtessa de Dia,

4
00:00:33,330 --> 00:00:37,534
a poet and composer from medieval southern France.

5
00:00:37,534 --> 00:00:41,405
It is the only surviving melody written by a woman

6
00:00:41,405 --> 00:00:44,107
from that entire tradition.

7
00:00:44,107 --> 00:00:46,510
So let me start with the question:

8
00:00:46,510 --> 00:00:50,180
who decides which voices survive?

9
00:00:50,180 --> 00:00:53,283
I never really thought about that until a few years ago,

10
00:00:53,283 --> 00:00:56,219
when I was working in a second-hand book market in London,

11
00:00:56,219 --> 00:01:01,525
and I found an encyclopedia that listed 6,000 women composers

12
00:01:01,525 --> 00:01:03,694
in the history of music.

13
00:01:03,694 --> 00:01:05,095
I could not believe it

14
00:01:05,095 --> 00:01:08,031
because at that time I think I could name five.

15
00:01:08,031 --> 00:01:10,367
But as soon as I started looking into their names

16
00:01:10,367 --> 00:01:13,971
and listening to this incredible music, I kid you not, I would not shut up.

17
00:01:13,971 --> 00:01:15,672
Well I haven’t shut up. I’m here.

18
00:01:15,672 --> 00:01:20,444
And I just am sharing this music with everybody all the time.

19
00:01:20,444 --> 00:01:22,879
In fact, please allow me.

20
00:01:22,879 --> 00:01:51,775
(Music)

21
00:01:51,775 --> 00:01:53,677
I mean, can you believe that?

22
00:01:53,677 --> 00:01:55,946
Did you hear all the classical strings?

23
00:01:55,946 --> 00:02:00,117
The Afro-American dances mixed with the romantic music?

24
00:02:00,117 --> 00:02:02,653
Well welcome to my husband’s life

25
00:02:02,653 --> 00:02:03,954
in 2018.

26
00:02:03,954 --> 00:02:05,522
(Laughter)

27
00:02:05,522 --> 00:02:10,093
Now, my husband, he’s a railway engineer,

28
00:02:10,093 --> 00:02:13,497
which means he's now the railway engineer

29
00:02:13,497 --> 00:02:17,167
who knows the most about women composers in the whole world.

30
00:02:17,167 --> 00:02:18,502
(Laughter)

31
00:02:18,502 --> 00:02:21,038
I know, it's not a very competitive field, but still.

32
00:02:21,038 --> 00:02:22,172
(Laughter)

33
00:02:22,172 --> 00:02:24,241
So one day he said to me,

34
00:02:24,241 --> 00:02:27,444
"Look, it's very nice that you're playing this music by these women

35
00:02:27,444 --> 00:02:29,746
at breakfast for like, I don't know, a year now.

36
00:02:29,746 --> 00:02:33,250
But you do understand that nothing is going to happen

37
00:02:33,250 --> 00:02:34,951
if you just tell me.

38
00:02:34,951 --> 00:02:36,286
I'm an engineer.

39
00:02:36,286 --> 00:02:38,388
So are you going to do something or what?"

40
00:02:38,388 --> 00:02:39,923
And I was like, "Fair enough."

41
00:02:39,923 --> 00:02:42,592
So I did. I made a list of these incredible women.

42
00:02:42,592 --> 00:02:46,830
I put it online, and within months, women from all over the world

43
00:02:46,830 --> 00:02:49,232
started writing to me and they said,

44
00:02:49,232 --> 00:02:51,234
"Thank you so much for what you're doing.

45
00:02:51,234 --> 00:02:54,304
I'm a composer. Could you please add me to your list?"

46
00:02:54,304 --> 00:02:56,707
And I tell you, this list continues to grow.

47
00:02:56,707 --> 00:02:59,076
It is now a foundation, and we are now uncovering

48
00:02:59,076 --> 00:03:02,379
over 18,000 women composers in the history of music.

49
00:03:02,379 --> 00:03:07,551
(Applause)

50
00:03:07,551 --> 00:03:12,189
Now this story makes absolutely no sense.

51
00:03:12,189 --> 00:03:14,758
I'm a professional musician.

52
00:03:14,758 --> 00:03:19,162
How is it possible that I spent my life training and performing

53
00:03:19,162 --> 00:03:24,968
without hearing about thousands of women who wrote beautiful music?

54
00:03:24,968 --> 00:03:28,338
Well when I started doing my own research, and I came across the data,

55
00:03:28,338 --> 00:03:29,606
then it became clear

56
00:03:29,606 --> 00:03:32,876
because, it turns out, that nearly

57
00:03:32,876 --> 00:03:36,179
90 percent of the music we hear across genres

58
00:03:36,179 --> 00:03:38,215
is written by men.

59
00:03:38,215 --> 00:03:40,951
That's a very big gap.

60
00:03:40,951 --> 00:03:44,955
Can you imagine all the amazing music

61
00:03:44,955 --> 00:03:47,958
we've been all missing all this time?

62
00:03:47,958 --> 00:03:55,632
But also that AI is now learning from that same silence.

63
00:03:55,632 --> 00:04:00,704
On one platform only, more than 50,000 AI-generated tracks

64
00:04:00,704 --> 00:04:04,374
are added every single day.

65
00:04:04,374 --> 00:04:07,844
The systems can only learn from the data available to them,

66
00:04:07,844 --> 00:04:10,547
but right now that data is missing.

67
00:04:10,547 --> 00:04:13,650
Half of humanity's creativity.

68
00:04:13,650 --> 00:04:18,655
Now I believe musicians should be writing music,

69
00:04:18,655 --> 00:04:19,956
not machines.

70
00:04:19,956 --> 00:04:23,527
(Applause)

71
00:04:23,527 --> 00:04:26,830
There are millions of talented people who can do that.

72
00:04:26,830 --> 00:04:30,834
Writing music fills our soul ... as humans.

73
00:04:30,834 --> 00:04:33,003
And to generate music from artists' work

74
00:04:33,003 --> 00:04:38,074
without their consent or proper remuneration is wrong.

75
00:04:38,074 --> 00:04:40,844
(Applause and cheers)

76
00:04:40,844 --> 00:04:44,080
But the technology is here to stay.

77
00:04:44,080 --> 00:04:47,617
So let's make sure all voices are heard, right?

78
00:04:47,617 --> 00:04:49,953
So how do we do that? It's actually quite easy.

79
00:04:49,953 --> 00:04:52,355
We must record the missing voices --

80
00:04:52,355 --> 00:04:54,591
the historical ones, but also the current ones

81
00:04:54,591 --> 00:04:55,926
who are not represented.

82
00:04:55,926 --> 00:05:00,363
Because a recording makes this music searchable, teachable,

83
00:05:00,363 --> 00:05:04,801
and part of the systems shaping what the world will hear next.

84
00:05:04,801 --> 00:05:09,773
It brings this music to classrooms, to radio stations, to streaming platforms

85
00:05:09,773 --> 00:05:13,577
so everybody in this room can play and share it.

86
00:05:13,577 --> 00:05:15,245
So here's my invitation.

87
00:05:15,245 --> 00:05:20,450
What if every tech company making AI music

88
00:05:20,450 --> 00:05:22,385
and anyone profiting from it

89
00:05:22,385 --> 00:05:26,923
would invest in making our musical catalog more complete?

90
00:05:26,923 --> 00:05:28,491
Can you imagine?

91
00:05:28,491 --> 00:05:31,862
What gets heard is chosen,

92
00:05:31,862 --> 00:05:36,666
and those choices will shape what the future will remember.

93
00:05:36,666 --> 00:05:39,870
So who decides which voices survive?

94
00:05:39,870 --> 00:05:44,207
Now more than ever, we do.

95
00:05:44,207 --> 00:05:48,211
And I want to leave you all with one woman's story.

96
00:05:48,211 --> 00:05:52,883
Ilse Weber was a poet, a singer

97
00:05:52,883 --> 00:05:56,653
who was imprisoned in the ghetto of Theresienstadt

98
00:05:56,653 --> 00:06:00,323
during World War II with her family.

99
00:06:00,323 --> 00:06:04,094
As she was there, she didn't write symphonies,

100
00:06:04,094 --> 00:06:10,533
but she wrote songs to entertain the elderly and the children.

101
00:06:10,533 --> 00:06:13,370
That's the power of music.

102
00:06:13,370 --> 00:06:17,073
And that was the gift of one woman.

103
00:06:17,073 --> 00:06:22,612
She and her son sadly died in Auschwitz,

104
00:06:22,612 --> 00:06:28,385
but her husband buried her manuscripts under a tool shed,

105
00:06:28,385 --> 00:06:32,856
and because of that, a few of her songs survived.

106
00:06:32,856 --> 00:06:34,758
This one

107
00:06:34,758 --> 00:06:36,693
is a lullaby.

108
00:06:36,693 --> 00:06:43,967
It's called "Wiegala".

109
00:06:43,967 --> 00:07:13,663
(Singing)

110
00:07:13,663 --> 00:07:14,898
(Song stops abruptly)

111
00:07:14,898 --> 00:07:31,581
[If you can’t hear this part, perhaps history never recorded it.]

112
00:07:31,581 --> 00:07:35,618
Artificial intelligence is learning.

113
00:07:35,618 --> 00:07:40,590
Let's teach it to remember the sounds of all of us.

114
00:07:40,590 --> 00:07:41,858
Thank you.

115
00:07:41,858 --> 00:07:45,858
(Applause)
